A.2.15. {"problem": "The relaxed invariant `deployed == true or harness_ok == true` no longer says that a verified request was deployed. A request whose deploy was refused (deployed false, harness_ok true, like R3) now satisfies it as 'verified'. The invariant cannot tell a dry run from a refused deploy, so it stops guarding what its name and original intent protect. The finished-deploys-record-the-outcome exception has the same weakness.", "where": "delivery.domain.yaml, invariants verified-requests-were-deployed and finished-deploys-record-the-outcome", "kind": "contradiction", "from": "llm"}
A.2.16. {"problem": "Verifying from 'built' moves the request to 'verified', which is terminal: preview, deploy and record_deployment are n/a and verify is denied as 'Already verified'. A dry-run-verified request can therefore never be deployed, and a new request is needed. The verified flag and stage also look the same as a real verification, so a dry run is indistinguishable from a verified-in-use deployment.", "where": "delivery.domain.yaml, transitions.DeliveryRequest built.verify and verified row", "kind": "behaviour", "from": "llm"}
A.2.17. {"problem": "Stale wording after the change. The glossary still defines 'verified' as 'A deployment a person has checked in use, after it happened.' The invariant id 'verified-requests-were-deployed' no longer matches its meaning. The purpose says nothing unverified may reach the environment, and 'verified' now covers undeployed builds.", "where": "delivery.domain.yaml, glossary.verified and invariant id verified-requests-were-deployed", "kind": "reference", "from": "llm"}
